        'markdown' => '**With the opening ceremony just around the corner, the Belgian Junior Team shifted gears today, moving from general site-familiarization to high-performance tactical training.**

===

The Swabian Alb continues to deliver interesting conditions. Today’s weather was characterized by high-based cumulus clouds and strong blue thermals in between. For our pilots, this was the perfect opportunity to practice "team flying"—staying connected in the air to find the best lift faster and cover more ground together.

The focus wasn\'t just on speed, but on precision. Navigating the local airspaces and calculating final glides over the hills of Aalen requires 100% concentration, and today was a successful dress rehearsal for the main event.

### Precision on the Grid
It’s not just about the flying; it’s about the routine. The ground crew has perfected the "morning rush"—getting the gliders water-ballasted, tow-out ready, and positioned on the grid in record time. In a competition where minutes can define the podium, having a smooth ground operation is our secret weapon.

### The Belgian Spirit
Back at the camp, the evening was spent analyzing flight logs. Seeing the traces of our gliders working together on the screen confirms what we already knew: Team Belgium is ready. The morale is high, the equipment is performing perfectly, and the pilots are "in the zone."

